---
description: >
  Three drift cycles documented. Cycles 1+2 (fixed-`brain`-key drift,
  post-move identity drift) are resolved. Cycle 3 — post-schema-collapse
  drift — is active. The schema-collapse refactor (Apr 24-26) retired
  the in-code backend registry entirely, but this spec's intent
  paragraph, outcomes, and Context still frame the install pipeline
  in terms of "the resolved backend's command/args underneath" and
  reference the retired `brain.status: invalid-path` state.
intent: >
  The schema-collapse settlement in `.aide/intent.aide` retired backend
  identity inference from the package: the in-code backend registry,
  matchers, and drivers are gone; the user's brain.aide IS the source
  of truth (no resolution step); the boot-state vocabulary collapsed
  to four states (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`)
  with `invalid-path` retired. This spec, stamped aligned in a prior
  cycle, now carries fossilized backend-resolution language in its
  intent paragraph (twice), outcomes.desired (once), outcomes.undesired
  (the half-wired-brain failure mode references `brain.status:
  invalid-path`), Context (once), and the parenthetical "obsidian
  today, others tomorrow." Each site is informational against today's
  contract but teaches a model the root spec has retired.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

### Active (alignment pass — post-schema-collapse drift)

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` intent paragraph (twice: "The brain MCP entry is keyed `brain` per the project-root settlement; the entry's command/args shape identifies the wired backend (obsidian today, others tomorrow)." and Context paragraph "with the resolved backend's command/args underneath"): the phrase "(obsidian today, others tomorrow)" and "the resolved backend's command/args underneath" both encode a backend-resolution model the root retired. Per `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] ("The in-code backend registry — matchers, drivers, identity inference — is fully retired. There is no module in this package whose job is to enumerate connectors, infer connector identity from MCP entries, or dispatch behavior on a connector enum.") the package no longer "resolves a backend"; the entry is derived from `parseBrainAide`-parsed `mcpServerConfig` bytes, byte-for-byte from the user's hand-written brain.aide. The intent should describe the entry as derived from the scaffolded brain.aide (per `provisionBrain/.aide`), not from a "resolved backend."
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15], outcomes.undesired[5] ("Any code path in this package that branches on connector identity"), and Strategy ("Brain configuration is user-owned, not code-owned... There is no module in this package whose job is to enumerate brains, infer which one a host has wired, or dispatch behavior on a connector enum"); `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` (single canonical reader); `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` (MCP entry derived via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.desired[7] ("Wiring the brain end-to-end from a resolved brainPath requires consumers to invoke the install service twice with distinct category values: once with `category: \"mcp\"` to apply the brain MCP prescription to .mcp.json (under the fixed `brain` key, with the resolved backend's command/args underneath)"): "the resolved backend's command/args underneath" carries the same retired backend-resolution frame. The MCP step's prescription is computed from the parsed brain.aide bytes (via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs in provisionBrain), not from a resolution step.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] (registry retired); `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired ("The MCP step's prescription.entry is derived from the scaffolded brain.aide bytes via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs, not from a hardcoded launcher constant").
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.undesired (half-wired-brain failure mode: "...pipeline agents that declare mcpServers: [brain] cannot reach the vault, and aide_info will still report \"brain.status: invalid-path\" even though the vault exists on disk"): the `invalid-path` state was explicitly retired in the schema collapse. Per `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[10] ("Boot reporter brain status states are reduced to `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` — the prior `invalid-path` state goes away with `rootPath`."), and per `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` outcomes.undesired ("Reintroducing the retired `invalid-path` state — under that name or any rename. Path validity is the launcher's job at MCP server startup; the detector does not stat any path"). The bullet should describe a different observable symptom (e.g., the brain MCP server fails to launch when invoked because the path is missing — the launcher's failure surface, not a boot-reporter state), or be removed if no symptom in the new four-state vocabulary cleanly captures the half-wired condition.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[10]; `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` outcomes.desired (four-state vocabulary) and outcomes.undesired (no `invalid-path`).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` Context paragraph ("...prescribes the brain MCP server entry (keyed `brain`, with the resolved backend's command/args underneath) for the consumer to merge into the project's MCP config alongside the aide server"): same backend-resolution frame as outcomes.desired[7]. Should describe the entry as derived from brain.aide via parseBrainAide.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[15] (registry retired); `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` (MCP entry derivation pipeline).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` Strategy paragraph "The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain`; legacy `obsidian` keys migrate through `provisionBrain`": the paragraph correctly names provisionBrain as the migration surface, but it does not yet acknowledge the load-bearing detail that the entry's command/args themselves now derive from the user-owned brain.aide via parseBrainAide. After the schema collapse, "MCP key is fixed at brain" is half the contract; the other half is "and its command/args are read from brain.aide, not constructed inline." This omission lets a reader infer the package still owns launcher bytes — exactly the model the schema collapse retired. (Suggested fix: extend the paragraph to name brain.aide as the source of truth and parseBrainAide as the reader, or reference provisionBrain's spec for the full pipeline.)
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[5] (brain.aide is the canonical source); `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` (single canonical reader); `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` (MCP entry derived via parseBrainAide).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/.aide` does not reference `.aide/config/` directory ownership. The root spec's outcomes.desired[8] establishes that `.aide/config/` is user-owned forever after first scaffold — `aide_init` and `aide_upgrade` must never overwrite, patch, or migrate any path inside it on subsequent runs. provisionBrain inherits this. The install service's own spec should cite this ownership boundary (or reference provisionBrain's spec for it) so consumers understand that the brain config category never returns `would-overwrite` for any path under `.aide/config/`. Not a contradiction with current outcomes — an omission of an inherited invariant the install service's contract depends on.
  Traces to: `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.desired[8] (`.aide/config/` is the canonical home of user-owned configuration; package-owned vs user-owned boundary is the directory path); `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` outcomes.desired (directory-wide ownership invariant for `.aide/config/`).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

### Resolved (prior alignment pass — fixed-`brain`-key drift)

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.desired ("The categories are: framework detection, methodology pointer stub, methodology doc hub, pipeline slash commands, pipeline agent definitions, pipeline skill templates, MCP server registration (aide), brain vault provisioning, MCP server registration (obsidian), and IDE configuration"): the category named "MCP server registration (obsidian)" is now "MCP server registration (brain)" per the fixed-key handoff. The category's purpose (write the brain-shaped entry, with obsidian-shaped command/args underneath) is unchanged; the canonical name of the category and the key it writes have changed.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled) -> "The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain`."
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.desired ("Wiring the brain end-to-end from a resolved brainPath requires consumers to invoke the install service twice with distinct category values: once with `category: \"mcp\"` to apply the obsidian MCP prescription to .mcp.json, and once with `category: \"brain\"` to scaffold vault directories..."): "the obsidian MCP prescription" pins the wrong key. The prescription is keyed `brain`, not `obsidian`. The two-call contract is otherwise still load-bearing.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.undesired ("A consumer invoking only `category: \"brain\"` with a resolved brainPath and declaring the brain fully wired. That single invocation scaffolds the vault directories and seeds content but leaves the obsidian MCP prescription unapplied — the .mcp.json vaultPath stays empty, pipeline agents that declare mcpServers: [obsidian] cannot reach the vault, and aide_info will still report 'brain.status: invalid-path' even though the vault exists on disk..."): the failure mode is correctly described but the labels "obsidian MCP prescription" and "mcpServers: [obsidian]" are stale. Should read "brain MCP prescription" and "mcpServers: [brain]" per the handoff.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` Context paragraph ("...without a vault and an Obsidian MCP server entry, agents that declare `mcpServers: [obsidian]` in their frontmatter silently lose brain access..."): same key-name and frontmatter-declaration drift. The post-handoff Agent frontmatter is `mcpServers: [brain]`.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled) — agent frontmatter sweep is in Remaining-work but the canonical contract is already settled.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` Context paragraph ("...prescribes the Obsidian MCP server entry for the consumer to merge into the project's MCP config alongside the aide server"): "the Obsidian MCP server entry" is the wrong canonical name — under the fixed-key invariant the entry is keyed `brain`.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` Strategy ("Brain vault is required; location is inherent; scaffolding is silent-on-create"): the paragraph itself is correct in structure, but the surrounding strategy section never reflects the fixed-key migration semantics — that the install service's `provisionBrain` helper now emits a `would-overwrite` step for legacy `obsidian`-keyed installs to migrate them to `brain`. The handoff explicitly names this as a one-place migration surface; the install-service spec should describe it (or at least reference `provisionBrain`'s todo for it) so consumers know migration is part of the install contract.
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` Done-this-session — three migration branches in `provisionBrain.buildBrainMcpStep`.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` Good example ("Cold start on a fresh project via the MCP-tool consumer"): "Your `.mcp.json` has `github` and `memory` configured. I'll add `aide` and `obsidian` alongside them. OK?" — the agent's user-facing prompt names the wrong key. Under the fixed-key world the prompt should read "I'll add `aide` and `brain` alongside them."
  Traces to: `.aide/handoff.aide` (settled).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

### Active (alignment pass — post-move drift)

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` frontmatter `scope: src/tools/init`
  Stale path — the module moved to `src/service/install/`. The scope field
  is a load-bearing identity claim about where this spec lives.
  Traces to: physical move (rename in git status from `src/tools/init/.aide`
  to `src/service/install/.aide`); ancestor commitment that the folder tree
  *is* the architecture (`.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[4] —
  "progressive disclosure in the folder tree, agent-readable names, `.aide`
  specs next to orchestrators").
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` intent paragraph: "aide_init is the
  bootstrap — the one command that turns a cold project into an AIDE-ready
  project."
  Identity drift — this module is no longer the MCP tool itself. It is the
  shared install service consumed by two callers: the `aide_init` MCP tool
  (`src/tools/init/`) and the new npx CLI (`src/cli/init/`). The spec
  conflates the service with one of its consumers, which means every
  cascading commitment downstream inherits the wrong actor identity.
  Traces to: physical reality (two consumers exist on disk); ancestor
  `src/.aide` -> outcomes.desired[3] ("Client-specific logic is confined to
  aide_init; every other tool is client-agnostic.") — the install service
  was previously *the* aide_init body, but now sits underneath aide_init as
  a callable, which means the "client-specific logic confined to aide_init"
  invariant needs the install service to be *not* aide_init.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.desired throughout: "The tool
  returns a structured JSON response...", "Re-running init on a fully
  initialized project...", "The tool's response is structured data the
  agent interprets..."
  Outcomes describe a single tool returning structured JSON to a single
  agent caller. The CLI consumer is not an agent and does not interpret a
  structured response — it executes the install service directly and
  surfaces a human-readable summary. The desired/undesired set was authored
  for the MCP-tool-only world and does not say which outcomes apply to
  which consumer.
  Traces to: ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[1] (two
  delivery channels; the spec was correct when "the tool" was the only
  delivery shape, but is now under-specified).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.desired bullet "When called with
  a `category` parameter, the tool writes all `would-create` files
  directly to disk itself..." and the surrounding bullets about "the agent
  never sees file content"
  These outcomes encode the MCP tool's two-call protocol (summary call ->
  category apply call) as if it were the install service's contract. The
  CLI does not have a "first call summary, second call apply" interaction
  shape — it runs the install end-to-end in one process. The contract
  needs to be re-stated: which invariants are about the install service
  (idempotency, prescription pattern, planner/apply split, status enum)
  and which are about the MCP tool's wire protocol on top of it.
  Traces to: physical reality (CLI invokes service directly without the
  two-call wire shape); ancestor `src/.aide` -> outcomes.undesired[3]
  ("Divergent behavior between MCP clients on the same input.") — this
  parity rule was scoped to MCP clients; the CLI is not an MCP client and
  the spec must say so explicitly.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` Bad examples section: "**Cold start on a
  fresh project**" example narrates "The agent calls `aide_init` with no
  category... The user runs `npx aidemd-mcp init` a week later..."
  The Good examples mix the MCP-tool agent shape and the CLI command shape
  in the same narrative without distinguishing them. The "npx aidemd-mcp
  init" mention is the only acknowledgment that a CLI exists in this
  spec — and it appears as a prose detail in an example, not as a
  first-class consumer in the intent or outcomes.
  Traces to: physical reality (CLI is a real consumer); ancestor
  `.aide/intent.aide` -> undesired[2] ("a standalone CLI that teaches" is
  forbidden as a delivery channel) — this is the load-bearing question
  the parent must resolve: is the CLI a third delivery channel, or a
  thin invocation of channel two? The spec needs to take a position so
  the bad-example test stays meaningful.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` References section: every path is
  `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/tools/init/...`
  Stale paths after the move. References are hints not contracts, but
  every entry in this list points at a file that no longer exists at the
  named path — a reader following the references reaches dead links and
  has to back-derive the new locations. Includes the parent reference
  `src/.aide` (still valid) and `src/tools/init/plan.aide`,
  `src/tools/init/todo.aide`, `src/tools/init/detectFramework/index.ts`,
  `src/tools/init/installAgents/plan.aide` (all moved).
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.undesired[3] ("a canonical doc that describes behavior the
  server does not actually deliver") — references that point at moved
  files describe a topology that no longer exists.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/service/install/.aide` outcomes.undesired[15]: "An init that
  appends the pointer stub on every run, fails the whole run on a single
  step failure, hardcodes vault paths, or reaches for the network. **All
  prior undesired outcomes from the original spec still apply.**"
  This bullet inherits invariants by reference rather than by enumeration
  — load-bearing under the old single-consumer model, dangerous now that
  the consumer set has expanded. "All prior undesired outcomes" needs to
  be re-grounded against the install-service-with-two-consumers shape so
  consumers know which invariants they each owe.
  Traces to: ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[1] ("Two
  delivery channels, each with one clear job") — invariants must be
  attached to clear owners; "applies to whatever the prior version was"
  is a forwarding pointer, not a commitment.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

### Cycle 3 (post-schema-collapse)

The schema-collapse refactor (Apr 24-26) was a multi-axis architectural
shift — backend registry retired, brain.aide schema collapsed to two
fields, `.aide/config/` directory ownership established, four-state boot
vocabulary, `invalid-path` retired. The trunk (`.aide/intent.aide`) and
the brain layer specs (`parseBrainAide`, `buildBrainState`,
`provisionBrain`) all updated together. This install-service spec, one
level above the brain layer, was not re-walked — its prior `aligned`
stamp survived the refactor, and its intent paragraph and outcomes
still teach the pre-collapse "resolve a backend" / "obsidian today,
others tomorrow" / "invalid-path" model. The pattern matches
`feedback_schema_collapse_amplifies_cascade.md`: multi-axis root
revisions cause multiplicative drift, and every spec in scope of the
shift needs re-walking even when its prior alignment was clean.

The fix is a `/aide:spec` revision of this spec's intent paragraph,
outcomes.desired[7], outcomes.undesired (the half-wired-brain bullet),
Context, and the brain-key Strategy paragraph — replacing
backend-resolution language with "derived from the user-owned
brain.aide via parseBrainAide" framing, retiring the `invalid-path`
reference, and citing the `.aide/config/` ownership boundary either
inline or by reference to provisionBrain.

### Cycle 2 (post-move)

1. `src/.aide` ("two delivery channels — handshake teaching surface and
   aide_init bootstrap") needs to widen its claim. Either the CLI is a
   thin invocation of channel two (and the spec says so explicitly),
   or it is a third channel (and the parent's `aide_init bootstrap`
   claim is replaced with `install service consumed by aide_init MCP
   tool and npx CLI`).
2. This spec needs to re-introduce itself as the install service, with
   outcomes scoped to the service contract (planner+apply, prescriptions,
   idempotency, status enum) and the consumer-shape outcomes (two-call
   wire protocol, agent-driven branching) clearly marked as MCP-tool
   responsibilities the service supports rather than service invariants.

What would have caught this earlier: a pre-merge alignment pass on any
commit that moves a folder containing `.aide` files, or that introduces
a new consumer of a previously single-consumer module. The git rename
itself (`R src/tools/init/.aide -> src/service/install/.aide`) is the
exact signal — a renamed `.aide` file should auto-flag the spec as
needing alignment review.
